narrative:
## Passing Information to Functions

Last lesson's `greet()` function was nice, but it always said the same thing. What if you want to greet different people? That's where **parameters** come in.

### Adding a Parameter

```xiom
fn greet(name: Str) {
  io.println("Hello, ");
  io.println(name);
  io.println("!");
}

fn main() {
  greet("Alice");
  greet("Bob");
  greet("Charlie");
}
```

Now `greet` has a parameter: `name: Str`. Inside the parentheses, you declare what information the function needs. When you call `greet("Alice")`, the string "Alice" flows into the `name` parameter, and the function uses it.

Run this program and you'll see personalized greetings for all three friends. One function, infinite names!

### Multiple Parameters

You can add more parameters with commas:

```xiom
fn introduce(name: Str, age: Int) {
  io.println(name);
  io.println(" is ");
  io.println(age);
  io.println(" years old.");
}

fn main() {
  introduce("Dana", 22);
}
```

`introduce` takes TWO ingredients: a name (Str) and an age (Int). Order matters — the first argument goes to `name`, the second to `age`.

